WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.270 --> 00:00:04.670
59. Body Language Across Cultures

2
00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:11.020
Do you know where my father is? J.D. asked her friend Karen.

3
00:00:11.740 --> 00:00:15.660
There, Karen said, and pointed at J.D.'s father with her finger.

4
00:00:16.700 --> 00:00:23.420
J.D. turned to Karen and frowned. Then she walked away. She didn't speak to Karen much for the rest

5
00:00:23.420 --> 00:00:29.820
of the party. Karen couldn't understand what went wrong. That's because Karen forgot about body

6
00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:36.060
language. We all know how difficult it can be to speak to people from other cultures,

7
00:00:36.620 --> 00:00:43.020
but there's also a language of movement called body language. For Karen from Canada,

8
00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:49.500
it was no problem to point at someone who is older than she is, but for J.D., it was very rude.

9
00:00:50.620 --> 00:00:53.900
Different cultures have many different rules about body language.

10
00:00:54.620 --> 00:01:01.900
Kissing is one important example. In France and much of Europe, you should kiss new friends on

11
00:01:01.900 --> 00:01:08.220
one or both cheeks. But in India and much of the Middle East, a man kissing a woman in public

12
00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:15.180
would be shocking. Think about how you treat little children. In many parts of the world,

13
00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:21.340
people often pat children on the head, but that would be unacceptable in most of Southeast Asia.

14
00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:26.300
You must also never show the soles of your feet in that part of the world,

15
00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:31.420
so you couldn't sit back at your desk with your feet up as it is okay in the United States.

16
00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:38.380
When you listen to a story, do you look the storyteller in the eye? In the United States,

17
00:01:38.380 --> 00:01:44.540
you should from time to time. In the Middle East, it is normal to make eye contact for a

18
00:01:44.540 --> 00:01:52.540
long period of time. But in Japan and Korea, that would be rude. Communication doesn't stop

19
00:01:52.540 --> 00:01:58.940
at our mouths. We use our whole bodies to communicate. Remember that there are as

20
00:01:58.940 --> 00:02:02.780
many rules for body language as for spoken language.
